### 🔑 Understanding Hash Functions

The hash functions you listed (SHA256, SHA1, SHA512, MD5) are algorithms that convert input data into a fixed-size string of characters, which is typically a unique digest that represents the original data. They are crucial for data integrity verification, password storage, and security applications.

The table below summarizes these common hash functions:

| Hash Function | Output Size | Security Status & Key Considerations |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **MD5** | 128 bits | **Considered cryptographically broken and unsuitable for further use.** Known for critical vulnerabilities and weak collision resistance. |
| **SHA1** | 160 bits | **No longer considered secure.** Vulnerabilities make it susceptible to collision attacks; recommended to replace it in security-sensitive applications. |
| **SHA256** | 256 bits | **Currently considered secure.** Part of the SHA-2 family; recommended for high-security applications like digital signatures and certificates. |
| **SHA512** | 512 bits | **Currently considered secure.** Also part of the SHA-2 family; offers stronger security due to a longer hash length. |

